import json,pathlib
r='frontier-40-geometry-braids-rep-27';p=pathlib.Path('research')/f'{r}-batch-25.pages.json';a=json.loads(p.read_text());src={'title':'The Stacks Project, Resolution of Surfaces, 54.7.8 and 54.8.8: relative duality import (proved locally in the projective regular-base case)','url':'https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/download/resolve.pdf'}
def row(id,title,deps,statement,strategy,level=0):return dict(id=id,kind='lemma',title=title,deps=deps,statement=statement,strategy=strategy,design_row='AG-BIR-1',local_addition=True,provenance={'statement':'literature-derived','proof':'ai-altered'},sources={'references':[src]},dependency_level=level)
h1=row('lem-relative-projective-space-regular-local-base-twisted-resolution','Finite twisted resolutions over a regular local base',['def-axiom-of-choice','lem-graded-section-module-finite-projective','lem-extend-sections-from-nonvanishing-open','thm-localisation-and-polynomial-extension-of-regular-rings','thm-projective-dimension-at-most-n-iff-the-nth-syzygy-is-projective','thm-nakayama-lemma','thm-exactness-of-sheaves-stalkwise','thm-affine-quasi-coherent-equivalence'],r'''Assume AC. Let $R$ be a regular Noetherian local ring of finite dimension $d$, $P=\mathbb P^N_R$, and $F$ a coherent sheaf on $P$. There is a finite resolution of $F$ by finite direct sums of twists $\mathcal O_P(m)$, of length at most $d+N+1$. Every bounded coherent complex on $P$ is perfect and belongs to the triangulated subcategory generated by these twists.''',r'''Set $S=R[x_0,\ldots,x_N]$, graded in the variables. The published graded-section finiteness supplier applies to this arbitrary Noetherian base: a sufficiently high truncated section module $M$ is finite graded over $S$. The section-extension argument identifies $\widetilde M=F$ on each standard open by $u/x_i^n\mapsto u/x_i^n$; truncation does not affect this localization because one can multiply numerator and denominator by a sufficiently high power of $x_i$. Resolve $M$ successively by finite graded free modules, using Noetherian kernels. The regular polynomial-ring global-dimension bound gives $\operatorname{pd}_S M\le d+N+1$, so the terminal syzygy $K$ is a finite graded projective $S$-module. Its quotient $K/S_+K$ is finite projective over the local ring $R$, hence free: lift a residue-field basis to a finite free cover by Nakayama, split the cover, and apply Nakayama to its kernel, which has zero residue quotient. Choose homogeneous lifts of a finite homogeneous $R$-basis. They define a finite graded free cover $G\to K$: its cokernel vanishes by bounded-below graded Nakayama (a least nonzero degree cannot equal a sum of positive-degree multiples). An ungraded projective splitting can be made degree zero by taking homogeneous components; its graded kernel is finite and is zero modulo $S_+$, so the same argument makes it zero. Thus $K$ is graded free. Sheafify the finite graded free resolution: localization and taking degree zero are exact, as is the affine associated-sheaf equivalence, so it becomes the claimed twist resolution. Finite truncation triangles give the statement for bounded coherent complexes. This adapts the field-only twisted-resolution proof at exactly its changed step: the degree-zero quotient is free over the **local regular base**, rather than a vector space.''')
h2=row('lem-relative-projective-space-derived-duality-regular-local-base','Relative derived duality on projective space over a regular local ring',['def-axiom-of-choice',h1['id'],'thm-cohomology-projective-space-twisting-sheaves','def-cup-product-sheaf-cohomology','thm-cech-computes-qc-cohomology-separated-scheme-affine-cover','thm-hom-tensor-adjunction-for-modules','thm-five-lemma-for-a-morphism-of-long-exact-sequences'],r'''Assume AC. Let $R$ be regular Noetherian local of finite dimension and $P=\mathbb P^N_R$. Write $W=\mathcal O_P(-N-1)$. Laurent residue gives $t:R\Gamma(P,W[N])\to R$. For every $K\in D^b_{\mathrm{Coh}}(P)$, evaluation followed by $t$ gives a natural quasi-isomorphism $R\Gamma(P,\mathcal R\!Hom_P(K,W[N]))\cong R\operatorname{Hom}_R(R\Gamma(P,K),R)$. It respects shifts, triangles and multiplication by homogeneous sections.''',r'''Relative twisting-sheaf cohomology over the arbitrary ring $R$ is the actual published theorem: its standard-cover Laurent monomial complex decomposes as a direct sum over exponent vectors. A vector with some, but not all, negative entries has a contractible simplex complex; all-nonnegative vectors contribute degree zero and all-negative vectors contribute degree $N$. The residue on the unique all-minus-one vector defines $t$. For any twist $K=\mathcal O(m)$, the monomial pairing $x^a\cdot x^{-a-1}\mapsto1$ identifies the finite free degree-zero cohomology with the dual of the corresponding top cohomology, and all other groups vanish; it is compatible with polynomial multiplication by direct multiplication of Laurent monomials. The monomial complexes have the described contractible summands, so their derived global sections are their finite-free cohomology shifted appropriately; no exact duality over a field is assumed. Derived cup/evaluation and ordinary tensor-Hom adjunction on bounded finite-projective representatives produce the natural map in the statement. It is a quasi-isomorphism for twists, finite sums and shifts by the preceding calculation. Both sides are contravariant triangulated functors (the right side is **derived** Hom, not ordinary dual of arbitrary cohomology), so cones and the five lemma propagate this to finite twist resolutions and finite truncation triangles supplied by the preceding helper. For $N=0$ it is ordinary perfect-complex biduality over $R$. Chain evaluation and the signed total differential give shift/triangle compatibility, and the monomial calculation gives the required trace normalization.''',1)
h3=row('lem-projective-regular-local-base-coherent-duality-by-embedding','Projective coherent duality over a regular local base',['def-axiom-of-choice','def-dualizing-complex-on-projective-cm-scheme',h2['id'],'lem-finite-closed-immersion-derived-coinduction-adjunction','lem-regular-quotient-dualizing-complex-and-biduality','thm-localisation-and-polynomial-extension-of-regular-rings'],r'''Assume AC. Let $R$ be regular Noetherian local of dimension $d$, let $X$ be projective over $R$, and fix $i:X\hookrightarrow P=\mathbb P^N_R$. Put $D_X=i^!(\mathcal O_P(-N-1)[N+d])$. Then $D_X$ is a dualizing complex on $X$, with coherent biduality. For every bounded coherent complex $K$ on $X$, trace/evaluation gives $R\Gamma(X,\mathcal R\!Hom_X(K,D_X))\cong R\operatorname{Hom}_R(R\Gamma(X,K),R[d])$. The pairing, not just its vector-space shadow, is canonical for the supplied embedding.''',r'''The affine charts of $P$ are regular finite-dimensional polynomial rings over $R$. On a chart with ring $A$, $X$ has ring $A/I$ and the ambient twist is invertible. The actual regular-quotient biduality supplier applies over this regular ring, hence $i^!$ produces a local dualizing complex with coherent biduality. Coinduction uses the natural quotient action and evaluation at one, so the chart complexes glue. Uniform finite twist resolutions over $P$ bound coherence degrees, giving a bounded coherent global complex. Closed-immersion internal Hom adjunction identifies $i_*\mathcal R\!Hom_X(K,D_X)$ with $\mathcal R\!Hom_P(i_*K,\mathcal O_P(-N-1)[N+d])$, and closed-immersion cohomology comparison identifies global sections with those on $X$. Apply the preceding relative projective-space duality, shifted by $d$, to obtain the displayed quasi-isomorphism. Its evaluation trace is obtained by the coinduction counit followed by Laurent residue, so it is the actual compatible composition pairing. Coherent biduality holds locally by the regular-quotient supplier, hence globally. This proves the required projective regular-base complex duality; concentration into a single dualizing **module** on a normal two-dimensional modification is a separate remaining helper, not asserted from this formula alone.''',2)
for h in [h1,h2,h3]:
 idx=next(i for i,x in enumerate(a[0]['items']) if x['id']=='thm-resolution-of-normal-surface-singularities');a[0]['items'].insert(idx,h)
p.write_text(json.dumps(a,indent=2)+'\n')
